/**
  @page examples-index Examples and tutorials
  @brief Guided numerical problems and application source examples.

  @tableofcontents

  The tutorials explain a problem, its discretization, the code that realizes
  it, and the interpretation of the result. Source examples extend that
  workflow to other models and backends.

  @section examples-index-start Start here

  1. @ref guides-getting-started-first-steps "Build a first program" to create
     a mesh and a finite element space.
  2. @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Solve the Poisson equation" to connect a weak
     form to assembly, a solver, and visualization.
  3. @ref examples-pdes-elasticity "Continue with linear elasticity" for a
     vector-valued field and boundary traction.

  The @ref guides-index "General concepts" guides supply the mathematical
  background. The @ref gallery "Gallery" collects visual results.

  @section examples-index-variational Before choosing a model

  A variational problem combines a trial space, a test space, forms, and
  boundary conditions. The @ref guides-variational "variational formulations guide" explains this construction; the @ref guides-solvers "solver guide"
  explains how the resulting matrix properties determine solver choice.

  @section examples-index-pdes Partial differential equations

  Begin with Poisson, then select a tutorial according to the field type,
  boundary conditions, or geometry needed for the problem.

  | Tutorial | Main idea |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | @ref examples-pdes-poisson | Scalar diffusion and homogeneous Dirichlet data |
  | @ref examples-pdes-dg-poisson | Discontinuous affine space, SIPG fluxes and convergence checks |
  | @ref examples-pdes-elasticity | Vector displacement and boundary traction |
  | @ref examples-pdes-helmholtz | Wave propagation and complex-valued fields |
  | @ref examples-pdes-stokes | Incompressibility, mixed spaces and pressure normalization |
  | @ref examples-pdes-navierstokes | Transient cavity flow driven by a moving lid patch |
  | @ref examples-pdes-periodic | Periodic boundary identification and a mean-value constraint |
  | @ref examples-pdes-surfacemesh | Tangential operators and refinement on a triangulated sphere |

  The @ref guides-weak-formulations "weak formulation guide" derives the
  forms; the @ref guides-solvers "solver guide" explains their algebraic
  requirements. The @subpage examples-pdes-index "PDE overview" provides
  the same tutorial sequence on a separate page.

  @section examples-index-io Input/output

  Select an example according to the data being exchanged. XDMF records
  fields and time-dependent snapshots for ParaView; MFEM and MEDIT examples
  demonstrate mesh and field exchange.

  | Tutorial | Output and checks |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | @ref examples-io-xdmf | Export a time-dependent vector field with its mesh |
  | @ref examples-io-mfem | Save and reload a mesh and scalar field |
  | @ref examples-io-medit | Create a mesh, save MEDIT and convert to MFEM |

  The @ref guides-io "I/O guide" explains format selection and field
  lifetimes. See also the @subpage examples-io-index "I/O overview".

  @section examples-index-geometry Mesh geometry

  Generated meshes provide a starting point for geometry and incidence
  operations without an input file.

  | Tutorial | Main idea |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | @ref examples-geometry-uniformgrid | Create structured meshes and set their physical dimensions |
  | @ref examples-geometry-connectivity | Inspect incidence relations between vertices, edges and cells |
  | @ref examples-interface-traces | Evaluate the two traces, jump and average on an interior facet |

  The @ref guides-meshes "mesh guide" covers traversal and queries, while
  the @ref guides-polytopes "polytope guide" explains reference elements
  and mappings. The @subpage examples-geometry-index "geometry overview"
  also links the distance-field examples below.

  @section examples-index-mmg Working with MMG

  MMG changes mesh topology to improve element shape, follow a size field or
  reconstruct an implicit domain. Construct fresh finite element spaces
  after remeshing and check required entities and labels at each stage.

  | Tutorial | Main idea |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | @ref examples-mmg-simple_io | Read and write MMG mesh data and check metadata |
  | @ref examples-mmg-casting | Work with MMG and Geometry mesh interfaces |
  | @ref examples-mmg-mesh_optimization | Improve element quality and inspect the resulting mesh |
  | @ref examples-mmg-mesh_adaptation | Adapt the background to a prescribed size field |
  | @ref examples-swift-background | Adapt a background, classify a circle and fit its interface with SWIFT |
  | @ref examples-mmg-behavior | Required entities, level-set cuts and preservation guarantees |

  The @subpage examples-mmg-index "MMG overview" collects these workflows.

  @section examples-index-density_optimization Density optimization examples

  Density optimization varies a material coefficient on a fixed domain.
  A state solve determines temperature, an adjoint supplies sensitivity,
  and a regularized update changes the density within its admissible interval.

  | Tutorial | Main idea |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | @ref examples-density_optimization-simp_temperature_minimization | SIMP conductivity, adjoint sensitivity and a projected material update |

  Begin with the @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Poisson solve". The thermal
  tutorial supplies computed density and temperature fields, objective and
  material histories, and the complete source. Its fixed iteration budget
  defines the recorded experiment; stationarity or a prescribed material
  budget requires an associated optimization criterion. The
  @subpage examples-density_optimization-index "density optimization overview"
  develops the state–sensitivity–update sequence.

  @section examples-index-shapeoptimization Shape optimization examples

  Shape optimization varies the material boundary of an elastic body.
  Compliance and an area penalty define the objective; a regularized shape
  derivative supplies the geometry update. Both tutorials use MMG and require
  a hydrated input mesh from the repository's Git LFS resources.

  | Tutorial | Main idea |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | @ref examples-shapeoptimization-simple_cantilever | Explicit boundary displacement followed by remeshing |
  | @ref examples-shapeoptimization-levelset_cantilever | Signed-field transport and body-fitted interface reconstruction |

  Start with the boundary-deformation example after the
  @ref examples-pdes-elasticity "elasticity tutorial". Compare compliance,
  area and objective at solved states, then inspect Jacobian signs and
  element quality after each update. The level-set tutorial records both
  increasing objective steps and a reproduced low-quality triangle, making
  step acceptance and geometric quality part of interpreting its fixed-budget
  run. The @subpage examples-shapeoptimization-index "shape optimization overview"
  explains the two representations and prerequisites.

  @section examples-index-solid Solid mechanics source examples

  The @ref Rodin::Solid "Solid" module provides hyperelastic constitutive
  laws and nonlinear integrators for large-deformation solid mechanics. The
  @ref Rodin::Variational::LinearElasticityIntegral "LinearElasticityIntegral" covers the small-deformation
  (linearized) case, while the Solid module handles finite-strain problems with
  constitutive laws such as NeoHookean, Saint-Venant-Kirchhoff, and
  Mooney-Rivlin:

  @m_class{m-scroll}

  @parblock
  | Tutorial | Method |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | @ref examples-pdes-elasticity "Linear cantilever beam" | Linear elasticity with @f$\sigma=\lambda(\operatorname{tr}\varepsilon)I+2\mu\varepsilon@f$ |
  | [Hyperelastic cantilever](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/Solid/CantileverBeam.cpp) | Transient Neo-Hookean model with @f$W=\frac{\mu}{2}(I_1-d)-\mu\ln J+\frac{\lambda}{2}(\ln J)^2@f$ |
  @endparblock

  @section examples-index-eikonal Distance and Eikonal

  The @ref Rodin::Eikonal "Eikonal" module solves @f$ F(x)|\nabla u| = 1 @f$ for positive propagation speed @f$F@f$
  via the Fast Marching Method, and the @ref Rodin::Distance "Distance" module
  wraps several distance-function computation strategies:

  | Tutorial | Method |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | @ref examples-eikonal-fmm | Fast marching with an exact point-source comparison and mesh refinement |
  | @ref examples-distance-normalization | Spalding–Tucker normalization of a signed Poisson field and geometric error checks |

  @section examples-index-petsc PETSc source examples

  The @ref Rodin::PETSc "PETSc" module enables distributed solvers and
  multi-field block systems. Examples include:

  @m_class{m-scroll}

  @parblock
  | Tutorial | Description |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | [Sequential Poisson](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/PETSc/PDEs/Seq_Poisson.cpp) | PETSc CG solver on a local mesh |
  | [MPI Poisson](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/PETSc/PDEs/MPI_Poisson.cpp) | Distributed mesh and PETSc KSP |
  | [Sequential Stokes](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/PETSc/PDEs/Seq_Stokes.cpp) | Mixed velocity, pressure and pressure-mean multiplier |
  | [Oseen flow](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/PETSc/PDEs/Seq_OseenFlow.cpp) | Transient Picard/Oseen linearization |
  | [Oseen lid-driven cavity](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/PETSc/PDEs/Seq_OseenLidDrivenCavity.cpp) | Transient cavity with Picard/Oseen linearization |
  | [Navier–Stokes](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/PETSc/PDEs/Seq_NavierStokes.cpp) | Transient nonlinear lid-driven cavity with PETSc SNES |
  @endparblock

  @see @ref guides-petsc "PETSc guide", @ref guides-mpi "MPI guide"

  @section examples-index-dg Discontinuous Galerkin

  Begin with two-sided traces, then assemble a complete interior-penalty solve.

  | Tutorial | Output and checks |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | @ref examples-interface-traces | One-sided values, jump and average on a two-cell mesh |
  | @ref examples-pdes-dg-poisson | Independent cell-local affine unknowns, SIPG fluxes, weak Dirichlet data, an affine patch test and four-level refinement |

  @section examples-index-integral Integral equation source examples

  The `Potential` operator enables integral
  equation formulations (boundary element methods). The kernel
  @f$ K(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) @f$ is convolved with the solution on the mesh:

  @m_class{m-scroll}

  @parblock
  | Tutorial | Method |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | [Newtonian potential](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/IntegralEquations/NewtonianPotential.cpp) | Scalar kernel @f$1/(4\pi\lVert\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{y}\rVert)@f$ |
  | [Elastic distribution](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/IntegralEquations/ElasticDistribution.cpp) | Navier @f$3\times3@f$ kernel |
  @endparblock

  @section examples-index-surfevol Surface evolution source examples

  The surface evolution examples demonstrate conormal advection — moving
  domain boundaries along their outward normal direction using level-set
  methods with MMG implicit-domain meshing:

  | Tutorial | Description |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | [Conormal advection](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/SurfaceEvolution/ConormalAdvection/Main.cpp) | Move the boundary along its conormal |
  | [Convergence study](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/SurfaceEvolution/ConormalAdvection/Test.cpp) | Parametric sweep over resolution and CFL |
  | [Plane evolution](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/SurfaceEvolution/ConormalAdvection/Plane.cpp) | Planar surface motion |

  @section examples-index-boundaryopt Boundary optimization source examples

  Advanced shape and topology optimization examples that combine complex-valued
  PDE solvers, adjoint methods, Hilbert space regularization, and MMG
  remeshing:

  @m_class{m-scroll}

  @parblock
  | Tutorial | Method |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | [Acoustic cloaking](https://github.com/cbritopacheco/rodin/blob/develop/examples/BoundaryOptimization/AcousticCloaking.cpp) | Complex Helmholtz solve, adjoint shape gradient and conormal advection |
  @endparblock

  */
